 "CET6-202506-1-Q36": {"anchor":"In the words of Robert Putnam, \"People may go to the library looking mainly for information, but they find each other there.\"","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q37": {"anchor":"Supporters of libraries as community builders claim that unless you are out there changing neighborhoods, you are not completing the work you are to do.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q38": {"anchor":"In a time when education is increasingly expensive, public libraries provide information and educational opportunities free for all people, regardless of their socio-economic status.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q39": {"anchor":"Like a major department store in a mall, libraries attract large numbers of people, creating economic opportunities for numerous businesses and organizations in the surrounding area.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q40": {"anchor":"But in an increasingly digital world, we see the role of libraries as community and cultural centers at times undervalued.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q41": {"anchor":"Free tutoring, homework help programs, and summer reading programs for kids and teens help bridge the economic divide that impacts students' academic performance.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q42": {"anchor":"An organized archive is a place where people can research their ancestry and immigration history, do environmental research, and more.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q43": {"anchor":"Despite the rising costs of concert and theater tickets, public library events (including concerts, author visits, and gallery displays) are often offered free of charge, enabling people of any income level to attend.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q44": {"anchor":"Libraries serve in these capacities and are more than just about information.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q45": {"anchor":"Public libraries, through such programs as The Human Library and Socrates Cafe, can help build small communities of difference that encourage people to interact with and learn from each other through dialogue.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q46": {"anchor":"His open letter stated that missing 10% of school days in a year for any reason—excused or unexcused—is a primary cause of low academic achievement.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q47": {"anchor":"We find that absences excused by a parent do little to harm children's learning.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q48": {"anchor":"Unexcused absences can be a powerful signal of how those out-of-school challenges affect children's academic progress.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q49": {"anchor":"These policies assume that missing school for any reason harms children academically because they are missing classroom instruction.","logic":""},
 "CET6-202506-1-Q50": {"anchor":"We instead suggest using unexcused absence from school as a signal to channel resources to the children and families who need them most.","logic":""},
